WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.290 --> 00:00:02.380
- [Kim] Hi, this is Kim from Khan Academy

00:00:02.380 --> 00:00:05.080
and today I'm learning
more about Article VI

00:00:05.080 --> 00:00:07.150
of the U.S. Constitution.

00:00:07.150 --> 00:00:09.600
Article VI is as we'll soon see

00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:12.320
kind of a constitutional grab bag.

00:00:12.320 --> 00:00:16.090
It covers debts,
religious tests for office

00:00:16.090 --> 00:00:18.140
and it establishes the Constitution

00:00:18.140 --> 00:00:19.910
as the supreme Law of the Land.

00:00:19.910 --> 00:00:23.885
To learn more about what binds
these diverse ideas together,

00:00:23.885 --> 00:00:26.330
I sought out the help of two experts.

00:00:26.330 --> 00:00:28.460
Kermit Roosevelt is a Professor of Law

00:00:28.460 --> 00:00:31.814
specializing in constitutional
law and conflict of laws

00:00:31.814 --> 00:00:34.360
at the University of
Pennsylvania Law School.

00:00:34.360 --> 00:00:36.860
And Michael Ramsey is Professor of Law

00:00:36.860 --> 00:00:39.940
and Director of International
and Comparative Law programs

00:00:39.940 --> 00:00:43.070
at the University of San
Diego's School of Law.

00:00:43.070 --> 00:00:44.470
So let's start out talking

00:00:44.470 --> 00:00:47.730
about the debts portion of Article VI.

00:00:47.730 --> 00:00:50.060
Professor Roosevelt, why were the framers

00:00:50.060 --> 00:00:52.200
so interested in debt?

00:00:52.200 --> 00:00:54.630
What was the historical
context that led them

00:00:54.630 --> 00:00:57.407
to explicitly address debt in Article IV?

00:00:57.407 --> 00:00:59.600
- [Professor Roosevelt] Well,
debt is important generally

00:00:59.600 --> 00:01:01.880
because nations often
need to borrow money.

00:01:01.880 --> 00:01:04.110
And specifically with the Constitution

00:01:04.110 --> 00:01:06.419
and the Articles of
Confederation, the U.S. government

00:01:06.419 --> 00:01:10.100
had been borrowing money to
pay for the Revolutionary War.

00:01:10.100 --> 00:01:11.960
So there was a question we're moving

00:01:11.960 --> 00:01:14.177
from a sort of loose confederation,

00:01:14.177 --> 00:01:16.302
almost like a treaty between nations,

00:01:16.302 --> 00:01:18.468
under the Articles of Confederation,

00:01:18.468 --> 00:01:21.270
to a single unitary country

00:01:21.270 --> 00:01:24.110
with a stronger national
government under the Constitution.

00:01:24.110 --> 00:01:26.380
And there's a question, is
it still the same country?

00:01:26.380 --> 00:01:28.470
Will the new United States pay the debt?

00:01:28.470 --> 00:01:29.737
So the old United States.

00:01:29.737 --> 00:01:31.830
- [Professor Ramsey] Now,
there's a general principle

00:01:31.830 --> 00:01:35.030
of international law that
a successor government

00:01:35.030 --> 00:01:37.530
undertakes the obligations
of the predecessor.

00:01:37.530 --> 00:01:41.090
So you wouldn't necessarily
think this would be a problem

00:01:41.090 --> 00:01:42.900
but I think they were
particularly concerned

00:01:42.900 --> 00:01:46.970
because the idea of a
republic was a somewhat new,

00:01:46.970 --> 00:01:51.070
and at least in the 18th
century a somewhat unusual one.

00:01:51.070 --> 00:01:54.910
And the change of a republican government

00:01:54.910 --> 00:01:57.830
might cause some worries in Europe

00:01:57.830 --> 00:01:59.430
where this money was owed.

00:01:59.430 --> 00:02:02.390
So I think they just wanted to
reassure all of the creditors

00:02:02.390 --> 00:02:05.890
that even if they were changing
their method of government,

00:02:05.890 --> 00:02:08.701
that that wasn't going to
effect any of the debts.

00:02:08.701 --> 00:02:10.930
- [Kim] What might have
happened had they decided

00:02:10.930 --> 00:02:12.406
not to pay those debts?

00:02:12.406 --> 00:02:13.740
- [Professor Roosevelt]
If they had decided

00:02:13.740 --> 00:02:16.100
not to pay the debts, then other countries

00:02:16.100 --> 00:02:18.090
would probably have been much less willing

00:02:18.090 --> 00:02:20.820
to lend money to the new United States.

00:02:20.820 --> 00:02:21.790
Because they might have thought,

00:02:21.790 --> 00:02:24.351
well you know another change
in government could occur.

00:02:24.351 --> 00:02:25.560
- [Professor Ramsey] There
was substantial question

00:02:25.560 --> 00:02:27.030
throughout the world
whether the United States

00:02:27.030 --> 00:02:30.480
would be able to survive in
the face of all the challenges

00:02:30.480 --> 00:02:32.912
that they had after
gaining their independence.

00:02:32.912 --> 00:02:37.840
So in order to make it
seem that the United States

00:02:37.840 --> 00:02:39.780
was a country that could be trusted,

00:02:39.780 --> 00:02:42.040
a country that could be
expected to stick around

00:02:42.040 --> 00:02:45.530
and not collapse in the chaos
or revert to colonial status,

00:02:45.530 --> 00:02:46.990
one of the most important things for them

00:02:46.990 --> 00:02:50.280
was to show that the
debts would be honored.

00:02:50.280 --> 00:02:51.950
Because a failure to honor debts

00:02:51.950 --> 00:02:55.329
would suggest that the
country did not in fact

00:02:55.329 --> 00:02:59.680
have true sovereignty and was
not prepared to be an actor

00:02:59.680 --> 00:03:02.051
on the international stage
that could be trusted.

00:03:02.051 --> 00:03:02.884
- [Professor Roosevelt]
Another interesting thing

00:03:02.884 --> 00:03:04.840
to contrast this to is
the treatment of debts

00:03:04.840 --> 00:03:06.270
after the Civil War.

00:03:06.270 --> 00:03:08.937
Where of course the United
States, the federal government,

00:03:08.937 --> 00:03:11.540
paid its own debts but there's a provision

00:03:11.540 --> 00:03:13.870
in the 14th amendment
explicitly repudiating

00:03:13.870 --> 00:03:15.100
the Confederate debt.

00:03:15.100 --> 00:03:17.515
So if you loaned money to the
Confederate States of America,

00:03:17.515 --> 00:03:19.320
you're never getting that back.

00:03:19.320 --> 00:03:21.763
Because we didn't treat
that as a valid government

00:03:21.763 --> 00:03:24.181
that would be continued going forward.

00:03:24.181 --> 00:03:25.797
- [Professor Ramsey] Another
thing that it illustrates

00:03:25.797 --> 00:03:29.060
is that the Constitution in some respects

00:03:29.060 --> 00:03:31.300
was a visionary document
that was concerned

00:03:31.300 --> 00:03:33.580
with the long term future
of the United States

00:03:33.580 --> 00:03:36.796
but in other respects, it
responded to very immediate,

00:03:36.796 --> 00:03:40.460
practical problems that the
framers faced in their day.

00:03:40.460 --> 00:03:42.610
They were thinking about
not just the future

00:03:42.610 --> 00:03:45.180
of the country for the ages,
they were thinking about that

00:03:45.180 --> 00:03:47.086
but they weren't thinking just about that.

00:03:47.086 --> 00:03:49.342
They were also thinking
about reassuring France

00:03:49.342 --> 00:03:52.660
with respect to the debts that
existed right at that moment.

00:03:52.660 --> 00:03:55.890
- [Kim] So there's a lot
going on in Article VI

00:03:55.890 --> 00:03:59.190
and specifically it talks
about the Constitution

00:03:59.190 --> 00:04:01.699
as the supreme Law of the Land.

00:04:01.699 --> 00:04:04.013
So what's important about that statement?

00:04:04.013 --> 00:04:05.550
- [Professor Roosevelt]
What's important about that

00:04:05.550 --> 00:04:09.200
is that it means the
Constitution is our highest law.

00:04:09.200 --> 00:04:13.620
It prevails over any other
kind of law in a conflict.

00:04:13.620 --> 00:04:15.068
So one thing that that means

00:04:15.068 --> 00:04:18.270
is the Constitution is
supreme over state law.

00:04:18.270 --> 00:04:20.270
And then the Constitution actually goes on

00:04:20.270 --> 00:04:22.570
to talk about that a little bit more.

00:04:22.570 --> 00:04:24.300
But it also means the
Constitution is supreme

00:04:24.300 --> 00:04:25.330
over federal law.

00:04:25.330 --> 00:04:27.610
So everyone is bound by the Constitution.

00:04:27.610 --> 00:04:30.462
The states can't go against
it, congress can't go past it,

00:04:30.462 --> 00:04:33.600
the president can't violate
constitutional restrictions.

00:04:33.600 --> 00:04:35.630
The Constitution is really the last word.

00:04:35.630 --> 00:04:37.878
It's the pinnacle or the keystone

00:04:37.878 --> 00:04:40.357
of the arch of American democracy.

00:04:40.357 --> 00:04:42.099
- [Professor Ramsey] And
that's why we can say

00:04:42.099 --> 00:04:44.770
that things are unconstitutional,

00:04:44.770 --> 00:04:48.310
that laws are unconstitutional
and therefore invalid.

00:04:48.310 --> 00:04:51.471
And most importantly, it's
why the Supreme Court can say

00:04:51.471 --> 00:04:56.061
that laws are
unconstitutional and invalid.

00:04:56.061 --> 00:05:01.061
It creates a superior
law that limits the laws

00:05:01.620 --> 00:05:04.200
that can be passed by the
other parts of the government.

00:05:04.200 --> 00:05:08.218
It creates a hierarchy
of laws and in doing so,

00:05:08.218 --> 00:05:11.951
it assures that we have
a single set of rules

00:05:11.951 --> 00:05:16.810
that applies to all the states
and to the federal government

00:05:16.810 --> 00:05:19.660
and it can't be changed
except by an amendment.

00:05:19.660 --> 00:05:21.530
Which is relatively difficult to do.

00:05:21.530 --> 00:05:22.930
There's a procedure in the Constitution

00:05:22.930 --> 00:05:24.380
for how you can amend the Constitution

00:05:24.380 --> 00:05:28.270
but until amending, the
Constitution as written

00:05:28.270 --> 00:05:30.690
is our superior law.

00:05:30.690 --> 00:05:35.690
And that was different from
the way that the framers,

00:05:36.010 --> 00:05:38.490
the rules the framers were used
to under the English system,

00:05:38.490 --> 00:05:41.190
where they didn't have
a written Constitution.

00:05:41.190 --> 00:05:44.100
They had an unwritten
Constitution but that Constitution

00:05:44.100 --> 00:05:46.934
was subject to change by Parliament.

00:05:46.934 --> 00:05:48.700
- [Kim] So has this supremacy

00:05:48.700 --> 00:05:51.523
of the Constitution been tested over time?

00:05:52.746 --> 00:05:54.250
- [Professor Roosevelt]
There haven't been a lot

00:05:54.250 --> 00:05:57.810
of claims that the
Constitution is not supreme.

00:05:57.810 --> 00:05:59.883
So generally speaking, everyone gives

00:05:59.883 --> 00:06:03.020
at least lip service to this idea.

00:06:03.020 --> 00:06:04.590
What's been tested is more the question

00:06:04.590 --> 00:06:07.440
of who gets to decide what
the Constitution means

00:06:07.440 --> 00:06:09.930
and when something conflicts with it.

00:06:09.930 --> 00:06:12.550
So if you want the Constitution

00:06:12.550 --> 00:06:14.760
and federal law to be supreme,

00:06:14.760 --> 00:06:16.890
probably you would want to have someone

00:06:16.890 --> 00:06:18.770
in the federal government deciding

00:06:18.770 --> 00:06:21.120
when there's a conflict
say with state law.

00:06:21.120 --> 00:06:23.573
And the forms that resistance
has taken over the years

00:06:23.573 --> 00:06:26.150
are more states saying

00:06:26.150 --> 00:06:28.490
not you know we can go
against the Constitution,

00:06:28.490 --> 00:06:29.980
we're above the Constitution,

00:06:29.980 --> 00:06:32.450
but states saying we don't
think what we're doing

00:06:32.450 --> 00:06:33.820
violates the Constitution.

00:06:33.820 --> 00:06:36.320
Right, and you the Supreme
Court, you think it does

00:06:36.320 --> 00:06:37.522
but you're wrong.

00:06:37.522 --> 00:06:39.640
- [Professor Ramsey] In the 19th century,

00:06:39.640 --> 00:06:41.500
just before the Civil War,

00:06:41.500 --> 00:06:44.730
the Supreme Court decided
in the Dread Scott case

00:06:44.730 --> 00:06:46.820
that African Americans
could not be citizens

00:06:46.820 --> 00:06:49.823
to the United States even
if they were freed slaves.

00:06:50.760 --> 00:06:55.339
And President Lincoln
believed that that was wrong.

00:06:55.339 --> 00:06:57.992
He said that there was
nothing in the Constitution

00:06:57.992 --> 00:07:02.070
that denied the ability
of them to be citizens.

00:07:02.070 --> 00:07:03.640
And he said that the Supreme Court

00:07:03.640 --> 00:07:06.690
had misinterpreted the Constitution

00:07:06.690 --> 00:07:09.620
and he would not accept
the Supreme Court's ruling

00:07:09.620 --> 00:07:10.730
in that regard.

00:07:10.730 --> 00:07:14.120
Later, in the 20th century,
the Supreme Court held

00:07:14.120 --> 00:07:16.800
that the Constitution barred segregation

00:07:16.800 --> 00:07:19.140
particularly in schools

00:07:19.140 --> 00:07:21.314
in the Brown versus
Board of Education case.

00:07:21.314 --> 00:07:25.210
But many southern governors
and other institutions

00:07:25.210 --> 00:07:27.210
throughout the south thought

00:07:27.210 --> 00:07:29.325
that the Supreme Court
had gotten that one wrong.

00:07:29.325 --> 00:07:33.710
And they refused to abide by
what the Supreme Court had said

00:07:33.710 --> 00:07:35.370
the Constitution means.

00:07:35.370 --> 00:07:36.650
- [Professor Roosevelt]
What they said was not

00:07:36.650 --> 00:07:39.280
the Constitution doesn't bind us

00:07:39.280 --> 00:07:41.050
but we know what the Constitution means

00:07:41.050 --> 00:07:43.230
better than you Supreme
Court, you're wrong,

00:07:43.230 --> 00:07:46.048
you're making this up, it's
political, it's not judging.

00:07:46.048 --> 00:07:48.380
- [Kim] Another thing that
Article VI talks about

00:07:48.380 --> 00:07:50.222
is religious tests.

00:07:50.222 --> 00:07:53.120
Why were the framers so interested

00:07:53.120 --> 00:07:55.480
in preventing religious
tests in government?

00:07:55.480 --> 00:07:58.923
What sort of historical evils
were they trying to prevent?

00:07:59.897 --> 00:08:00.730
- [Professor Roosevelt]
So this is connected

00:08:00.730 --> 00:08:03.520
to the basic idea of the
separation of church and state.

00:08:03.520 --> 00:08:04.452
And you separate church and state

00:08:04.452 --> 00:08:07.080
really to protect both of those things.

00:08:07.080 --> 00:08:09.300
So you want to protect
religion from being corrupted

00:08:09.300 --> 00:08:10.723
by political considerations

00:08:10.723 --> 00:08:13.236
but you also want to protect
your political system

00:08:13.236 --> 00:08:16.847
from being a battle ground
between rivaled religions.

00:08:16.847 --> 00:08:19.440
- [Professor Ramsey] So
where this comes from is

00:08:19.440 --> 00:08:21.155
that in England, they had had a series

00:08:21.155 --> 00:08:23.640
of what they called Test Acts.

00:08:23.640 --> 00:08:27.960
And what the Test Acts did was it required

00:08:27.960 --> 00:08:31.090
that for people to be eligible
for government offices

00:08:31.090 --> 00:08:35.350
that the people had to be
members of the Church of England.

00:08:35.350 --> 00:08:38.700
And that other religious groups,

00:08:38.700 --> 00:08:41.060
they were barred by the Test Acts

00:08:41.060 --> 00:08:42.957
from holding government office.

00:08:42.957 --> 00:08:47.957
So actually, many of
those minority religions,

00:08:48.100 --> 00:08:50.120
many adherence of those ended up coming

00:08:50.120 --> 00:08:53.420
to the American Colonies
to gain some measure

00:08:53.420 --> 00:08:55.330
of religious freedom.

00:08:55.330 --> 00:08:57.544
The pilgrims were an example of that.

00:08:57.544 --> 00:09:02.090
There was a Catholic colony in Maryland.

00:09:02.090 --> 00:09:06.070
And just generally speaking,
many of the people,

00:09:06.070 --> 00:09:08.807
many of the colonists who came over

00:09:08.807 --> 00:09:12.160
were people who were not part

00:09:12.160 --> 00:09:15.040
of the main established church in England.

00:09:15.040 --> 00:09:18.320
And so you can see why
they would not want to have

00:09:18.320 --> 00:09:20.060
something like the Test Acts.

00:09:20.060 --> 00:09:21.980
And they wanted to make clear

00:09:21.980 --> 00:09:24.580
that in the new national government

00:09:25.659 --> 00:09:28.412
that any religion, or no religion

00:09:28.412 --> 00:09:33.150
would be allowed for
government office holders.

00:09:33.150 --> 00:09:34.010
- [Kim] Do you think it's true

00:09:34.010 --> 00:09:36.480
that we don't have
religious tests or oaths

00:09:36.480 --> 00:09:37.770
in the United States?

00:09:37.770 --> 00:09:40.120
How about the practice
of swearing on a Bible

00:09:40.120 --> 00:09:42.477
during the presidential inauguration?

00:09:42.477 --> 00:09:43.740
- [Professor Roosevelt] Well
the practice of swearing

00:09:43.740 --> 00:09:46.160
on a Bible is very
interesting as is the fact

00:09:46.160 --> 00:09:48.675
that when the president
recites the oath of office,

00:09:48.675 --> 00:09:50.982
every president going
back to George Washington

00:09:50.982 --> 00:09:54.530
has added on to the end
of it, so help me God.

00:09:54.530 --> 00:09:55.930
There's actually an
oath in the Constitution

00:09:55.930 --> 00:09:59.020
the president has to swear to preserve,

00:09:59.020 --> 00:10:00.483
protect and defend the Constitution

00:10:00.483 --> 00:10:03.370
but the Constitution
doesn't say so help me God.

00:10:03.370 --> 00:10:05.800
The presidents just add
that on on their own.

00:10:05.800 --> 00:10:08.140
And actually that sort
of illustrates the way

00:10:08.140 --> 00:10:11.023
in which the Constitution treats religion.

00:10:11.023 --> 00:10:14.400
Which is it can't be part of government

00:10:14.400 --> 00:10:16.190
in an official sense.

00:10:16.190 --> 00:10:18.620
But we know that members of
government are also people

00:10:18.620 --> 00:10:21.068
and they have religious beliefs
that are important to them

00:10:21.068 --> 00:10:25.260
and we don't demand that
they exclude religion

00:10:25.260 --> 00:10:27.149
from their lives, we just demand

00:10:27.149 --> 00:10:30.150
that it be separated from
government authority.

00:10:30.150 --> 00:10:33.740
So you can swear on a
Bible if you want to,

00:10:33.740 --> 00:10:35.690
you don't have to.

00:10:35.690 --> 00:10:37.530
You can swear on some
other religious book.

00:10:37.530 --> 00:10:38.640
We had a member of congress

00:10:38.640 --> 00:10:40.628
take an oath of office on a Quran.

00:10:40.628 --> 00:10:44.850
So individual government
officials are allowed to

00:10:44.850 --> 00:10:49.340
include religion in so far as
it's about them personally.

00:10:49.340 --> 00:10:50.570
You know, what you think is appropriate

00:10:50.570 --> 00:10:52.110
to mark this occasion.

00:10:52.110 --> 00:10:54.960
What solemnifies this oath for you.

00:10:54.960 --> 00:10:55.887
You can do that.

00:10:55.887 --> 00:10:59.660
But we can't require
it and they can't make

00:10:59.660 --> 00:11:01.990
the exercise of their
power religious in nature.

00:11:01.990 --> 00:11:05.130
So you can't, you know
as a government official,

00:11:05.130 --> 00:11:08.400
exercise your power on religious grounds.

00:11:08.400 --> 00:11:10.870
- [Kim] Something that
strikes me about Article VI

00:11:10.870 --> 00:11:14.200
is that is addresses so
many different things.

00:11:14.200 --> 00:11:16.760
Do you have a sense of why

00:11:16.760 --> 00:11:21.100
debts and constitutional
supremacy and religious tests

00:11:21.100 --> 00:11:23.203
are all in one article?

00:11:24.724 --> 00:11:26.320
- [Professor Ramsey] The
Article VI, as you said,

00:11:26.320 --> 00:11:27.930
is a little bit of a grab bag.

00:11:27.930 --> 00:11:30.680
It's not entirely clear
how these different pieces

00:11:30.680 --> 00:11:32.810
of Article VI relate to each other.

00:11:32.810 --> 00:11:35.300
And I think they were just
things that the framers

00:11:35.300 --> 00:11:38.740
wanted in the Constitution
and didn't know for sure

00:11:38.740 --> 00:11:40.099
where else to put them.

00:11:40.099 --> 00:11:41.070
- [Professor Roosevelt]
I'm not exactly sure

00:11:41.070 --> 00:11:42.833
why the debts are there.

00:11:43.930 --> 00:11:46.319
If I had to say something about Article VI

00:11:46.319 --> 00:11:49.120
it would be it's sort of the glue

00:11:49.120 --> 00:11:52.240
that holds the constitutional
architecture together.

00:11:52.240 --> 00:11:56.740
So maybe the debts are in
there to explain the continuity

00:11:56.740 --> 00:11:58.650
between the U.S. government

00:11:58.650 --> 00:12:00.110
under the Articles of Confederation

00:12:00.110 --> 00:12:02.014
and U.S. government
under the Constitution.

00:12:02.014 --> 00:12:04.950
Then the supremacy clause explains

00:12:04.950 --> 00:12:06.600
how all of the different parts

00:12:06.600 --> 00:12:09.670
of the federal system are
supposed to fit together.

00:12:09.670 --> 00:12:11.460
And what the supremacy clause is saying

00:12:11.460 --> 00:12:14.500
is the Constitution is above all of them.

00:12:14.500 --> 00:12:16.180
The Constitution connects them all.

00:12:16.180 --> 00:12:18.586
Everyone has to abide by the Constitution.

00:12:18.586 --> 00:12:20.560
And it tells you, you
know the Constitution

00:12:20.560 --> 00:12:22.563
is the highest law, then
you've got federal law

00:12:22.563 --> 00:12:24.880
and then below that is state law

00:12:24.880 --> 00:12:26.270
so that if there's a conflict

00:12:26.270 --> 00:12:27.590
between federal law and state law,

00:12:27.590 --> 00:12:28.820
the federal law is gonna win.

00:12:28.820 --> 00:12:31.420
And then the last part of Article VI

00:12:31.420 --> 00:12:33.520
is sort of doing the same thing.

00:12:33.520 --> 00:12:35.970
Because what holds a country together?

00:12:35.970 --> 00:12:39.610
What binds people into a single people?

00:12:39.610 --> 00:12:41.480
In a lot of countries at
the time of the founding,

00:12:41.480 --> 00:12:42.370
it was religion.

00:12:42.370 --> 00:12:43.615
Right, religion was the glue

00:12:43.615 --> 00:12:45.500
that held the society together

00:12:45.500 --> 00:12:46.916
and if you weren't a
member of that religion,

00:12:46.916 --> 00:12:49.814
you were an outsider, you
were a second class citizen,

00:12:49.814 --> 00:12:51.493
you would be shunned

00:12:51.493 --> 00:12:55.370
and not given equal rights in some ways.

00:12:55.370 --> 00:12:58.271
The last clause of Article VI
says something sort of similar

00:12:58.271 --> 00:13:00.730
about America except it explicitly says

00:13:00.730 --> 00:13:02.890
it's not religion that binds us together.

00:13:02.890 --> 00:13:04.483
Right, no religious tests can be required

00:13:04.483 --> 00:13:07.390
but you do have to take an oath.

00:13:07.390 --> 00:13:08.860
What do you have to pledge to support?

00:13:08.860 --> 00:13:10.950
You have to pledge to
support the Constitution.

00:13:10.950 --> 00:13:12.551
So there again is telling
you the Constitution

00:13:12.551 --> 00:13:14.440
is what we all have in common.

00:13:14.440 --> 00:13:15.686
That's what makes us Americans.

00:13:15.686 --> 00:13:19.720
That really is the glue that
binds our society together.

00:13:19.720 --> 00:13:22.332
- [Kim] So we've learned
that Article VI is,

00:13:22.332 --> 00:13:24.770
as Professor Roosevelt put it,

00:13:24.770 --> 00:13:27.280
the glue that binds the country together.

00:13:27.280 --> 00:13:28.420
In assuming the debts

00:13:28.420 --> 00:13:30.786
from the era of the
Articles of Confederation,

00:13:30.786 --> 00:13:35.260
Article VI established the
continuity of U.S. government.

00:13:35.260 --> 00:13:38.657
It also placed the
Constitution, not religion,

00:13:38.657 --> 00:13:41.910
as the supreme law of the United States.

00:13:41.910 --> 00:13:43.670
To learn more about Article VI,

00:13:43.670 --> 00:13:45.660
visit the National Constitution Center's

00:13:45.660 --> 00:13:47.075
Interactive Constitution

00:13:47.075 --> 00:13:49.160
and Khan Academy's resources

00:13:49.160 --> 00:13:50.760
on U.S. government and politics.

